                <pb facs="0661" n="652" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg652"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M)/119: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Chargé in
                        France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Murphy</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-07-20T14:00:00-04:00">July 20, 1940—2
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>119. Department’s 108, July 18, 6 p.m. The following from American Consul,
                    Saigon, dated July 19: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Reference is made to Reed’s telegram July 18, 7 p.m. relating to ore.
                            French naval authorities at Saigon have just informed me that they have
                            received instructions from French Admiralty to release to the Soviet
                            authorities the wolfram and antimony unloaded from <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Selenga</hi> upon evidence of purchase by Soviet
                            Government; Admiralty merely takes the position that cargo discharged
                            from Soviet ship on advice of contraband committee could not be object
                            of sales transaction.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
